body:
***These are functions that array objects have*** But node lists *do not* have them.
These functions each take a function as a parameter, and that function is invoked on each element.
```js
const drivers = [
				{id : 3, name : "hamilton"},
				{id : 4, name : "Vertapon"},
				{id : 6, name : "vettel"}
];
```

1)  `.forEach`:  **Important**
```js
drivers.forEach(d => {alert(d.name)})
```
3) `.find`: Finds the *first* element in the array that matches the criteria **Important**
```js
const d = driver.find(function(d) {
			return d.id < 5;
})
// or using arrow syntax
const d = drivers.find(d => d.id < 5);
```
Note that if no criteria match, it will be undefined. (or maybe null, but who cares)

3) `.filter`: Finds *all* occurrences that match a criteria. **Important**
```js
const found = drivers.filter(d => d.id < 5)
```
then `found` would be an [[Array]] of the first two objects. If no criteria match, it will be an empty array. **BE CAREFUL WITH THIS** because `[]` is `true`.
4) `.reduce`: Converts a single attribute to a scalar
```js
const sum = drivers.reduce((prev, d) => prev + d.id);
//sum would 13
```

5) `.map`:  Is used to transform one array into a different array **Important**
```js
const list = drivers.map(d => "<li>" + d.name + "</li>");
```
So `list` will be an [[Array]], that looks like this:
`["<li>Hamilton</li>", "<li>Vertapon</li>", "<li>Vettel</li>"]`

**example:**
```js
const list = driver.map(d => {
					const li = document.createElement("li");
					li.textContent = d.name;
					return li;
				});
//list is an array of element nodes.
list.forEach(n => {parent.appendChild(n)});
```

6) `.sort`: returns a sorted copy of the array
```js
const sorted = drivers.sort((a,b) => {
				if (a.id < b.id) return -1 else return 1;
			})
// or using the ternary operator
const sorted = driver.sort((a,b) => (a.id < b.id) ? -1 : 1);
```
